A||||5||GoCartGuy||Oil Weights?||||1||
Z||000000||GoCartGuy||04-29-2001||11:15 PM||||Ok I know alot of you recomend using heavier than the 5W-30 oil in your Fiero, but here's my problem.  I tried that this winter at the recommendation of my friend, I put in 10W-30 and got a seriously fluctuating oil pressure on the gauge till the car warmed up.  I drained it and put back in 5W-30 and the problem went away.  My question is whether or not I should use the 10W-30 in warmer weather and 5W-30 in cooler weather or could I be having a serious problem?  I already know I burn a bit of oil, nothing serious, I don't really have to add any between the 3,000 mile changes, maybe half a quart.  I'm just wondering what could cause this difference.  BTW the car is an 85 GT auto.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000001||grinthock||04-29-2001||11:19 PM||||I was told by the FieroShop toronto to use 20W50 in the summer and 10W30 in the winter, protects the engine better, especially because I drive it hard.||||reg||14
Z||000002||saleenfiero||04-30-2001||01:40 AM||||I've been thinking, does 20-50 sap much more energy (as in use more gas) than say 5-30?  Seems like it would<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||04-30-2001||03:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by saleenfiero:<BR><B>does 20-50 sap much more energy (as in use more gas) than say 5-30?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I consider that a particularly viscous accusation  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Yep, thicker oil tends to consume more energy.  More losses in pumping the oil, plus greater friciton in the bearings.  But you knew that.<P>Much more?  Depends on how you define "much," but if you keep close track of fuel mileage, I suspect it might be detectable.  <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000004||Old Lar||04-30-2001||04:32 PM||||I used 10-30w once and saw much higher oil pressure reading (80)on the guage.  I went back to the Pontiac recommended 5-30w, pressure stays at 60. I don't push the car, mostly highway driving and still get 30+ mpg with the GT after 14 years.<P>I used the heavier weight oils in an old oil burner Ford and blew the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||04-30-2001||06:06 PM||||as far as I know, the more pressure the better up to a certain point anyway. I use 5w50 Castrol all year round. Thats with a turbo. Ive had no engine problems for 3yrs, burns no oil at all, my oil  pressure is 75 above 1500 rpm, 50 at idle, both with hot engine.<P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>I used 10-30w once and saw much higher oil pressure reading (80)on the guage.  I went back to the Pontiac recommended 5-30w, pressure stays at 60. I don't push the car, mostly highway driving and still get 30+ mpg with the GT after 14 years.<P>I used the heavier weight oils in an old oil burner Ford and blew the engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
